<Base title="Do you need a VPN on public Wi-Fi?" description="A practical public Wi-Fi security checklist: what HTTPS already protects, when a VPN helps, and what to do about fake hotspots and captive portals." slug="/learn/vpn-on-public-wifi">
<div class="wrap"><Crumbs items={[{label:'Privacy fundamentals',href:'/learn'},{label:'Public Wi-Fi safety',href:'/learn/vpn-on-public-wifi'}]} />
<article class="narrow"><h1>Do you need a VPN on public Wi-Fi?</h1>
<p class="hub-intro">Not automatically. HTTPS already protects the content of most modern web connections. A VPN can reduce what the hotspot operator sees and protect traffic between your device and a VPN server, but it cannot make an unsafe login page legitimate or prevent you from joining the wrong network.</p>
<h2>Start with the actual risks</h2>
<p>A public network is controlled by someone other than you. Other guests may be connected, its administrator may log activity, and an attacker could set up a hotspot with a familiar name. That does not mean everyone in a café can read your bank password: a properly configured HTTPS connection encrypts it in transit. The more realistic risks include joining an impersonated hotspot, ignoring a browser certificate warning, using an outdated device, opening an HTTP page, or signing into a malicious lookalike site.</p>
<p>A hotspot operator can generally see that your device connected and can observe some connection metadata. How much destination information is exposed depends on encrypted DNS, connection protocols, and the sites and apps involved. They should not be able to read the contents of a valid HTTPS connection. A VPN hides more destination detail from the hotspot by carrying traffic in an encrypted tunnel, but transfers some trust to the VPN provider. It is one layer, not a substitute for careful network selection.</p>
<h2>A quick decision guide</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Checking ordinary HTTPS sites on a current device:</strong> a VPN is optional. Keep the browser updated and do not bypass certificate warnings.</li><li><strong>Using an unfamiliar hotel or airport network regularly:</strong> a reputable VPN can reduce visibility to the local operator. A personal cellular hotspot may be an even simpler option if data and reception allow.</li><li><strong>Using sensitive work systems:</strong> follow your organization's instructions. A corporate VPN or zero-trust access tool is not interchangeable with a consumer VPN.</li><li><strong>Visiting a site that still uses HTTP:</strong> do not enter sensitive information. A VPN protects the connection only as far as its exit server, not the HTTP leg to the website.</li><li><strong>Seeing a fake-looking login page or certificate warning:</strong> stop. A VPN does not authenticate the hotspot or the page you are viewing.</li></ul>
<h2>Before you join</h2>
<p>Confirm the official network name with the venue, ideally from a posted sign or a staff member rather than the list of nearby networks. A name that looks almost right is not proof of ownership. Disable automatic joining for open networks if your device supports it. If you have a cellular plan and do not need the venue's network, using mobile data avoids that particular shared Wi-Fi entirely, although your mobile carrier remains a party to the connection.</p>
<p>Install system and browser updates when you have a trusted connection. Turn on your device firewall and use a screen lock. Sharing settings matter too: switch off file sharing or network discovery on public networks, and use the “public network” profile on operating systems that offer one. These steps address local-network exposure that a commercial VPN may not fully prevent.</p>
<h2>Captive portals: connect in the right order</h2>
<p>Many hotels and airports require a captive portal to accept terms or enter a room code before internet access works. A VPN may fail to connect until that portal is completed. Join the network, verify that the portal belongs to the venue, complete only the necessary sign-in, and then turn on the VPN if you use one. Some portals open an unencrypted local page to begin the process; do not submit a reused password or payment card to an unexpected form. If a portal demands an app installation or a browser certificate profile, ask staff to verify the process before continuing.</p>
<p>Once connected to a VPN, check that it says connected rather than assuming that opening the app was enough. If a hotspot session expires, the VPN may disconnect. A kill switch can help block traffic during a drop, but behavior differs by device, app and configuration. Test the setting yourself with an ordinary browsing session before relying on it for sensitive work. If your network requires a corporate VPN, use the one your organization specifies.</p>
<h2>While you browse</h2>
<p>Pay attention to the exact domain in the address bar. HTTPS tells you that the connection to that domain is encrypted; it does not tell you that a deceptive domain is trustworthy. Use saved bookmarks or your password manager to reach important accounts rather than following sign-in links displayed by the hotspot. A password manager that refuses to fill on a lookalike domain can provide a useful warning, although it is not infallible.</p>
<p>Use multifactor authentication for email and financial accounts. Prefer passkeys or an authenticator app where available over relying only on passwords. Avoid installing unexpected extensions or device profiles presented as a condition for accessing a café's internet. If a page says that your VPN must be disabled, consider whether the task can wait until you have mobile data or a trusted connection. Some legitimate networks restrict VPNs, but that restriction is a reason to reassess your options, not a reason to ignore other warning signs.</p>
<h2>After you leave</h2>
<p>Forget the network if you do not expect to use it again, or disable automatic reconnection. This reduces the chance of joining a similarly named hotspot later. Review any new profiles, apps or certificates installed during the session; remove anything you cannot identify, following your organization's rules on managed devices. If you entered credentials on a suspicious page, change that password from a trusted connection, end active sessions where possible, and enable multifactor authentication. Contact your bank promptly if payment details may have been exposed.</p>
<h2>What a VPN does not solve</h2>
<p>A VPN will not prevent a phishing site from collecting a password you type into it. It will not hide your identity from a site where you sign in, clean malware from a device, make outdated software safe, or remove all tracking cookies. It also cannot guarantee that traffic from every app is tunneled if split tunneling is enabled. If your goal is to protect sensitive content on the web, HTTPS remains important even while the VPN is on. Read our <a href="/learn/vpn-vs-https">VPN versus HTTPS explainer</a> for the distinction.</p>
<p>A VPN provider's own privacy claims deserve scrutiny. Look for a clear policy, a transparent ownership history and independently published audits with a defined scope. “No logs” is not an absolute technical guarantee. Avoid assuming that a free service is safe merely because it displays a lock icon; ask how it funds operations and handles data. Conversely, paying for a subscription is not proof of good security.</p>
<h2>A practical checklist to save</h2>
<ol><li>Verify the Wi-Fi name with the venue; avoid automatic connections.</li><li>Update your device, use its firewall, and turn off unnecessary sharing.</li><li>Complete the legitimate captive portal before trying to start a VPN.</li><li>Use HTTPS, verify important domains and never dismiss certificate errors casually.</li><li>If using a VPN, confirm the connection and understand how its kill switch behaves.</li><li>Use multifactor authentication and avoid entering credentials on suspicious pages.</li><li>Forget the network or turn off auto-join when you leave.</li></ol>
<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>A VPN can be useful on a network you do not control, particularly when you want to reduce the operator's view of your destinations. It is not mandatory for every HTTPS browsing session, and it does not turn a hostile network into a trusted one. The strongest approach is layered: verify the hotspot, keep software current, insist on valid HTTPS, use strong account security, and use a VPN or mobile data where your circumstances justify it. If you want to check where a VPN sends DNS queries, see our <a href="/guides/vpn-dns-leak">DNS leak guide</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Further reading:</strong> <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/how-safely-use-public-wi-fi-networks" rel="noopener noreferrer">FTC advice on public Wi-Fi</a> and <a href="https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/use-strong-passwords" rel="noopener noreferrer">CISA's account-security guidance</a>. Guidance here is educational and not a claim that any individual hotspot has been tested.</p>
